请再说一遍？

406
00:18:16.310 --> 00:18:18.710
You are like a terrible person.
你就像个糟糕透顶的人。

407
00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:21.310
These guys care about you.
这些人很在乎你。

408
00:18:21.410 --> 00:18:23.630
And you're just using them.
而你却在利用他们。

409
00:18:24.970 --> 00:18:27.890
Again, the food was very good.
话说回来，这顿饭很好吃。

410
00:18:29.030 --> 00:18:31.030
And then Angela has a little smile.
然后安吉拉露出了一个淡淡的微笑。

411
00:18:31.730 --> 00:18:33.710
Well, Ange, Lauren G.
安吉，来自马萨诸塞州的

412
00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:35.390
from Massachusetts wrote in.
劳伦·G.来信了。

413
00:18:35.550 --> 00:18:38.450
And this is also her favorite Kevin moment.
这也是她最喜欢的凯文的高光时刻。

414
00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:43.170
Not only that, Lauren said, Kevin is one of my favorite characters.
不仅如此，劳伦说，凯文是她最喜欢的角色之一。

415
00:18:44.390 --> 00:18:48.010
Lauren thinks that that moment is a humanizing moment.
劳伦认为那一刻非常有人情味。

416
00:18:48.330 --> 00:18:55.030
And it shows that Kevin is not just the office goofball, but that he loves people and he stands up for them.
这表明凯文不仅仅是办公室里的活宝，他还热爱他人，并为他们挺身而出。

417
00:18:55.390 --> 00:18:55.610
Yeah.
是啊。

418
00:18:55.990 --> 00:18:58.690
And Lauren, Collider agrees with you.
劳伦，Collider网站也同意你的看法。

419
00:18:58.690 --> 00:19:06.690
They actually put out an article in April of 2023 asking if Kevin was actually the heart of the office.
他们在2023年4月发表了一篇文章，探讨凯文是否其实是办公室的核心。

420
00:19:06.970 --> 00:19:07.150
Aw.
噢。

421
00:19:07.730 --> 00:19:08.210
Yeah.
是啊。

422
00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:17.710
And this is where we get into kind of the evolution of the character, going from a guy who does silly one-liners to having so much more depth.
这就是我们开始探讨这个角色的演变过程，从一个只会说傻乎乎的俏皮话的人，变成了拥有更多内涵的角色。

423
00:19:17.710 --> 00:19:27.590
The article points out that while Kevin may not be the smartest character, his simplicity and his genuine desire for happiness really resonated with audiences.
文章指出，虽然凯文可能不是最聪明的角色，但他的单纯以及对幸福的真挚渴望确实引起了观众的共鸣。

424
00:19:28.210 --> 00:19:33.470
Kevin is portrayed as a loyal friend who often lacks any kind of agenda.
凯文被塑造成一个忠诚的朋友，通常没有任何算计。

425
00:19:33.470 --> 00:19:37.950
He just seeks laughter and connection rather than personal gain.
他只是寻求快乐和人际连接，而不是个人利益。

426
00:19:38.290 --> 00:19:46.650
And the article points out that while Kevin wanted to be liked, Michael kind of needed to be liked at like an obsessive level.
文章还指出，虽然凯文希望被人喜欢，但迈克尔对被人喜欢的渴望已经达到了着迷的程度。

427
00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:53.670
And you also have characters like Dwight, Oscar, and Angela who really needed to feel superior to everybody.
而且你还有像德怀特、奥斯卡和安吉拉这样的角色，他们真的很需要一种高人一等的感觉。

428
00:19:54.110 --> 00:19:57.830
Jim was always kind of trying to impress Pam or make her laugh.
吉姆总是试图给帕姆留下好印象或者逗她笑。

429
00:19:57.990 --> 00:20:02.090
But of all the characters, Kevin really just wants to live in the moment.
但在所有角色中，凯文真的只是想活在当下。

430
00:20:02.830 --> 00:20:12.070
Collider also points out that when Kevin accidentally found out that Oscar was having an affair with Angela's husband, he is the one who is able to keep the secret.
Collider还指出，当凯文无意中发现奥斯卡和安吉拉的丈夫有染时，他是那个能够保守秘密的人。

431
00:20:17.170 --> 00:20:22.090
And that if he does make a crass joke, it's never to be mean or cruel.
而且如果他开了一个粗俗的玩笑，也绝对不是出于恶毒或残酷。

432
00:20:22.330 --> 00:20:26.750
And that, in fact, it really causes him pain to hurt anyone.
事实上，伤害任何人都会让他感到痛苦。

433
00:20:27.370 --> 00:20:39.410
And I wanted to point out one of my other favorite Kevin moments that I think really speaks to this humanizing piece of him that Brian Baumgartner played so well.
我想指出我另一个最喜欢的凯文时刻，我认为它真正体现了他富有人情味的一面，布莱恩·鲍姆加特纳把这个角色演得太棒了。

434
00:20:39.650 --> 00:20:41.710
And it's that moment in Chair Model.
那就是在《椅子模特》那一集里的时刻。

435
00:20:42.070 --> 00:20:44.830
When he gets our parking spaces back.
当他帮我们把停车位要回来的时候。

436
00:20:45.130 --> 00:20:46.970
And he has that talking head.
他有段面对镜头的独白。

437
00:20:47.230 --> 00:20:50.570
Yeah, where he says, it's just nice to win one.
对，他说，能赢一次感觉真好。

438
00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:54.270
Yeah, and he has a little bit of a tear up in his eye.
对，而且他眼眶里有点含着泪水。

439
00:20:54.750 --> 00:20:55.070
Yeah.
是啊。

440
00:20:55.630 --> 00:20:57.890
So that's my deep dive into Kevin.
这就是我对凯文的深度剖析。

441
00:20:58.070 --> 00:21:02.950
There are so many other like funny one-liners that I didn't even get to.
还有好多其他有趣的俏皮话，我甚至都没时间讲。

442
00:21:03.190 --> 00:21:05.990
Screen Rant did a whole list of fan favorites.
Screen Rant网站整理了一整份粉丝最爱列表。

443
00:21:06.710 --> 00:21:09.390
I can tell you some of them because they are fun.
我可以跟你分享一些，因为它们很有趣。

444
00:21:09.570 --> 00:21:10.490
Oh, let's hear them.
哦，快讲给我们听听。

445
00:21:10.490 --> 00:21:16.290
From the incentive when he says, me think why waste time say lot word when few word do trick.
来自《激励奖》那一集，他说，我为啥要浪费时间说那么多字，少说几个字就行了。

446
00:21:17.270 --> 00:21:20.730
From the finale, well, well, well, well, well.
来自大结局那一集，嗯，嗯，嗯，嗯，嗯。

447
00:21:20.970 --> 00:21:22.130
That's six wells.
那是六个“嗯”。

448
00:21:22.490 --> 00:21:23.410
Yeah, I love that.
对，我太喜欢那个了。

449
00:21:23.410 --> 00:21:29.390
From viewing party when he says, I wanted to eat a pig in a blanket in a blanket.
来自《观影派对》那一集，他说，我想把裹在毯子里的猪肉卷再裹在毯子里。

450
00:21:30.470 --> 00:21:40.290
Then also, this one is one of my favorites from Christmas party when he says mini cupcakes, as in the mini version of regular cupcakes, which is already a mini version of a cake.
还有这个，也是我最喜欢的片段之一，来自《圣诞派对》那一集，他说迷你纸杯蛋糕，也就是普通纸杯蛋糕的迷你版，而普通纸杯蛋糕本来就是蛋糕的迷你版。

451
00:21:40.290 --> 00:21:42.750
Like, honestly, where does it end with you people?
说实话，你们这些人到底什么时候才是个头？

452
00:21:43.830 --> 00:21:49.330
I love, like, it takes a lot to piss Kevin off, I feel like, but that did it.
我特别喜欢——把凯文惹毛其实挺不容易的我觉得，但那次是真的做到了。

453
00:21:49.330 --> 00:22:01.550
And while the point of my deep dive was to point out the more tender and, I guess, sensitive parts of Kevin, I'm going to end with this clip.
虽然我做深度剖析的目的是为了指出凯文比较温柔、而且我猜也比较敏感的那一面，但我最后要用这段剪辑收尾。

454
00:22:01.550 --> 00:22:04.810
This is Kevin from PDA.
这是来自PDA的凯文。

455
00:22:05.610 --> 00:22:10.950
I don't know if anyone else feels this way, and don't get me wrong, I love Michael and Holly.
我不知道是不是还有其他人也有同感，别误会，我爱迈克尔和霍莉。

456
00:22:11.690 --> 00:22:15.530
And maybe I'm being overly sensitive, but the PDA.
也许是我太敏感了，但那个PDA。

457
00:22:15.830 --> 00:22:17.370
Yes, the freaking PDA.
对，该死的PDA。

458
00:22:17.750 --> 00:22:18.590
Thank you.
谢谢。

459
00:22:20.030 --> 00:22:22.130
Obviously, so happy for them both.
显然，为他们俩感到非常高兴。

460
00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:23.010
I don't know, guys.
我不知道啊，伙计们。

461
00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:25.470
I, for one, enjoy watching them.
我个人挺喜欢看他们的。

462
00:22:25.590 --> 00:22:26.310
No, stop.
不，住手。

463
00:22:26.650 --> 00:22:27.030
Just don't.
别这样。

464
00:22:27.630 --> 00:22:28.190
Can I finish?
我能说完吗？

465
00:22:28.450 --> 00:22:28.970
Is that okay?
可以吗？

466
00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:37.030
I was saying I enjoy watching them because it makes me horny.
我是说我喜欢看他们是因为这让我觉得很色。

467
00:22:37.090 --> 00:22:37.330
Oh.
噢。

468
00:22:40.010 --> 00:22:43.090
He fights so hard for, like, can I finish?
他拼命地在争取，比如，我能说完吗？

469
00:22:43.170 --> 00:22:43.570
I know.
我知道。

470
00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:44.230
Can I finish?
我能说完吗？

471
00:22:44.430 --> 00:22:49.750
Like, maybe he's going to say something, like, maybe sentimental or, like, I'm happy for them.
比如，也许他要说点什么，比如，也许挺深情，或者，比如，我为他们高兴。

472
00:22:49.750 --> 00:22:50.890
Like, new love.
比如，新恋情。

473
00:22:50.890 --> 00:22:53.190
We should let them explore this time.
我们这次应该让他们自己去探索。

474
00:22:54.170 --> 00:22:55.830
And then he ends with that.
结果他最后来了这么一句。

475
00:22:55.950 --> 00:22:57.170
But it makes him horny.
但就是让他觉得很色。

476
00:22:57.270 --> 00:22:58.010
Oh, gosh.
天哪。

477
00:22:58.350 --> 00:22:59.670
That was really good.
这真的太棒了。

478
00:23:00.350 --> 00:23:06.590
Well, I love that our writers made all of our characters so layered.
嗯，我很喜欢我们的编剧把所有角色都写得这么有层次感。

479
00:23:06.970 --> 00:23:12.390
It was such a gift to get to play characters like this on a sitcom.
能在情景喜剧里扮演这样的角色真是一份大礼。

480
00:23:12.930 --> 00:23:13.450
Really?
真的吗？

481
00:23:13.870 --> 00:23:14.190
Yeah.
对。

482
00:23:14.190 --> 00:23:19.090
You know, they were always willing to give us things that would surprise the audience to play.
你知道，他们总是愿意给我们一些会让观众感到惊喜的内容去演。

483
00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:19.550
And us.
还有我们。

484
00:23:19.550 --> 00:23:20.150
And us.
还有我们。

485
00:23:20.330 --> 00:23:20.470
Yeah.
对。

486
00:23:21.610 --> 00:23:23.950
All right, Angela, I'm handing it over to you.
好了，安吉拉，交给你了。

487
00:23:24.110 --> 00:23:27.310
I think you have a very fun deep dive area of intereSt.
我觉得你有一个非常有趣的深度剖析兴趣领域。

488
00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:28.250
I do.
是的。

489
00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:31.870
My deep dive area of interest is so funny.
我深度剖析的兴趣领域太搞笑了。

490
00:23:32.150 --> 00:23:33.230
It's such a long title.
这个标题好长。

491
00:23:34.170 --> 00:23:38.610
Was inspired by this fan question sent in from Alexander G.
灵感来自德克萨斯州休斯顿的亚历山大·G发来的粉丝提问。

492
00:23:38.910 --> 00:23:39.810
in Houston, Texas.
在休斯顿，德克萨斯州。

493
00:23:40.730 --> 00:23:47.430
Alexander wrote and said, I have heard a lot of theories that Kevin is secretly a genius and is committing fraud with company money.
亚历山大写道：我听过很多理论，说凯文其实是个天才，一直在用公司的钱进行欺诈。

494
00:23:47.430 --> 00:23:54.450
There have been some hints in some episode, like in season three, when Martin Nash explains why he went to prison.
某些集数里有一些暗示，比如第三季中马丁·纳什解释他为什么入狱时。

495
00:23:54.450 --> 00:23:57.290
I want to know y'all's thoughts on this theory.
我想知道大家对这个理论有什么看法。

496
00:23:57.690 --> 00:24:02.810
Well, Alexander, there are lots of lots of fan theories about this.
那么，亚历山大，关于这个有许许多多的粉丝理论。

497
00:24:02.810 --> 00:24:05.110
And it sent me down a rabbit hole.
这让我陷入了一个兔子洞。

498
00:24:05.290 --> 00:24:09.310
I found so many moments on the show that support the secret genius theory.
我在剧中发现了超级多支持天才秘密理论的时刻。

499
00:24:09.310 --> 00:24:15.050
So my title is Kevin Malone, dot, dot, dot, secret genius.
所以我的标题是：凯文·马龙，点、点、点，秘密天才。

500
00:24:15.050 --> 00:24:15.170
Yes.
没错。

501
00:24:15.650 --> 00:24:18.130
Not dot, dot, dot, dot, dot.
不是点、点、点、点、点。

502
00:24:18.350 --> 00:24:18.690
No.
不是。

503
00:24:18.870 --> 00:24:19.030
No.
不是。

504
00:24:19.270 --> 00:24:20.070
Because just three dots.
因为只有三个点。

505
00:24:20.390 --> 00:24:20.750
Just three dots.
只有三个点。

506
00:24:20.870 --> 00:24:21.090
Okay.
好吧。

507
00:24:21.670 --> 00:24:27.710
Which, by the way, my kids have told me, my teenagers, that doing the dot, dot, dot is a very old person thing.
顺便说一句，我的孩子们曾告诉我，我的十几岁的孩子们，打出点、点、点是非常老年人的行为。

508
00:24:28.550 --> 00:24:32.670
I've heard any punctuation in a text is like an old person thing.
我听说短信里的任何标点符号都像是老年人的行为。

509
00:24:32.770 --> 00:24:35.870
Any capitalization, any periods.
任何大写字母，任何句号。

510
00:24:37.970 --> 00:24:41.550
I can't live in that world where we aren't punctuating things.
我无法生活在一个我们不给事情加标点符号的世界里。

511
00:24:41.930 --> 00:24:42.590
So sorry.
所以很抱歉。

512
00:24:42.690 --> 00:24:43.870
We were like, what are you talking about?
我们当时想，你在说什么？

513
00:24:43.950 --> 00:24:45.910
And then they went through our family text thread.
然后他们翻看了我们的家庭群聊记录。

514
00:24:46.330 --> 00:24:54.350
And the one that really made us all laugh is that Josh texted the family text thread and said, coming home from the gym, dot, dot, dot.
真正让我们所有人都大笑的是，乔什在家庭群里发消息说，刚从健身房回来，点、点、点。

515
00:24:55.170 --> 00:24:57.610
And the kids were like, dot, dot, dot, dot, what?
孩子们当时就说，点、点、点、点，什么？

516
00:24:58.030 --> 00:25:00.350
Wait, he didn't put anything after dot, dot, dot?
等等，他在点、点、点后面什么都没加吗？

517
00:25:00.690 --> 00:25:02.810
Coming home from the gym, dot, dot, dot.
刚从健身房回来，点、点、点。

518
00:25:03.510 --> 00:25:07.010
And he was like, I just wanted you guys to know I'm coming home from the gym.
而他说，我只是想让你们知道我要从健身房回来了。

519
00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:15.970
I add a lot of dot, dot, dots in my text messages to sort of help you read it the way I would want you to.
我在短信里加了很多点、点、点，好帮你按照我希望的方式去阅读它。

520
00:25:16.310 --> 00:25:18.490
For me, it's like I'm moving thoughts here.
对我来说，这就像是在这里转换思绪。

521
00:25:18.810 --> 00:25:18.930
Yeah.
对。

522
00:25:19.070 --> 00:25:19.630
I know.
我知道。

523
00:25:19.790 --> 00:25:27.170
I'm kind of like, I feel like as an actor, I appreciate a lot of punctuation because it helps me get a feel for the dialogue.
我有点觉得，作为一名演员，我很欣赏大量的标点符号，因为这有助于我对对话产生语感。

524
00:25:27.170 --> 00:25:30.930
And on The Office, there were a lot of dot, dot, dots in our scripts.
而在《办公室》里，我们的剧本里有很多点、点、点。

525
00:25:31.150 --> 00:25:31.370
I know.
我知道。

526
00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:37.090
They wanted to, like, suggest pauses and transitions from one thought to another.
他们想要暗示停顿以及从一个想法到另一个想法的过渡。

527
00:25:37.690 --> 00:25:40.030
So I guess I love a lot of punctuation.
所以我猜我很喜欢大量的标点符号。

528
00:25:40.350 --> 00:25:42.570
Well, it means you're old when you text.
好吧，发短信时这样就意味着你老了。

529
00:25:42.710 --> 00:25:42.930
Oh.
哦。

530
00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:49.210
Also, my kid said that Josh is like, he's the king of dot, dot, dot, but I'm the queen of emojis.
另外，我的孩子说乔什就像是，他是点、点、点之王，而我是表情符号女王。

531
00:25:49.730 --> 00:25:49.950
Oh.
哦。

532
00:25:50.510 --> 00:25:50.750
Yeah.
是啊。

533
00:25:50.790 --> 00:25:51.590
Thumbs up, heart.
大拇指向上，红心。

534
00:25:51.870 --> 00:25:52.710
Lady dancing.
跳舞的女士。

535
00:25:53.510 --> 00:25:55.490
Yeah, emojis mean you're old, too, right?
对啊，表情符号也意味着你老了，对吧？

536
00:25:55.630 --> 00:25:55.830
Yeah.
是啊。

537
00:25:56.410 --> 00:25:57.750
The young and stone emoji?
那个年轻而坚定的表情符号？

538
00:25:58.270 --> 00:25:59.070
I don't know.
我不知道。

539
00:25:59.250 --> 00:26:00.090
I don't know either.
我也不知道。

540
00:26:00.210 --> 00:26:00.490
Okay.
好吧。

541
00:26:00.630 --> 00:26:00.750
Anyway.
总之。

542
00:26:00.750 --> 00:26:01.930
We don't know because we're old.
我们不知道，因为我们老了。

543
00:26:02.050 --> 00:26:02.570
We wouldn't know.
我们不会知道的。

544
00:26:02.710 --> 00:26:03.230
We wouldn't know.
我们不会知道的。

545
00:26:03.350 --> 00:26:03.570
Okay.
好吧。

546
00:26:03.570 --> 00:26:03.930
Sorry.
抱歉。

547
00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:04.770
Where was I?
我刚才说到哪了？

548
00:26:04.770 --> 00:26:08.110
Oh, Kevin Malone, dot, dot, dot, secret genius.
哦，凯文·马龙，点、点、点，秘密天才。

549
00:26:08.330 --> 00:26:08.510
Yes.
是的。

550
00:26:08.750 --> 00:26:09.510
Please go on.
请继续。

551
00:26:09.770 --> 00:26:13.070
Let's start with something that, Jenna, you and Kevin have in common.
让我们先从一个你和凯文共同的特点开始，珍娜。

552
00:26:14.250 --> 00:26:14.770
What?
什么？

553
00:26:15.030 --> 00:26:17.670
You're both fantastic poker players.
你们俩都是极好的扑克玩家。

554
00:26:18.070 --> 00:26:19.230
Oh, yes.
哦，是的。

555
00:26:19.870 --> 00:26:20.390
Yes.
没错。

556
00:26:20.730 --> 00:26:24.790
We learn about Kevin's poker playing in Casino Night, Season 2.
我们在第二季的《赌场之夜》里知道了凯文会玩扑克。

557
00:26:25.070 --> 00:26:26.710
Here's the moment I'm talking about.
这就是我所说的那一幕。

558
00:26:26.890 --> 00:26:27.890
You referred to it.
你提到过它。

559
00:26:28.050 --> 00:26:29.590
Let's hear this Kevin talking head.
让我们听听凯文这段单人访谈。

560
00:26:30.270 --> 00:26:36.930
I won the 2002 $2,500 No Limit Deuce to Seven Draw Tournament at the World Series of Poker
我赢得了2002年世界扑克大赛中2500美元奖金的无限注二到七换牌锦标赛

561
00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:37.470
in Vegas.
在拉斯维加斯。

562
00:26:38.530 --> 00:26:41.470
So, yeah, I'm pretty good at poker.
所以，是的，我扑克打得挺好的。

563
00:26:42.930 --> 00:26:43.450
Yeah.
是啊。

564
00:26:43.830 --> 00:26:45.650
A little bit of a flex there.
这可真有点凡尔赛了。

565
00:26:46.410 --> 00:26:51.750
And, you know, after watching that Kevin talking head, I thought, what makes a good poker player?
而且，你知道，看完凯文的单人访谈后，我在想，什么样的人才算是个优秀的扑克玩家？

566
00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:53.350
What do they all have in common?
他们都有什么共同点？

567
00:26:54.190 --> 00:26:56.450
Here's what the internet has to say, folks.
各位，互联网上是这么说的。

568
00:26:56.630 --> 00:26:56.890
Okay.
好的。

569
00:26:56.890 --> 00:27:04.270
They are disciplined, patient, confident, have a mental toughness, and a strong understanding
他们自律、有耐心、自信、心理素质过硬，并且对博弈论

570
00:27:04.270 --> 00:27:08.090
of game theory and the math behind poker strategy.
以及扑克策略背后的数学原理有着深刻的理解。

571
00:27:08.590 --> 00:27:14.770
They can manage their bankroll effectively and have strong analytical skills, decision-making,
他们能够有效地管理资金，具备强大的分析能力、决策能力，

572
00:27:14.970 --> 00:27:20.090
plus the ability to read opponents, adapt to different playing styles, and know how to
再加上洞察对手的能力、适应不同打法风格的能力，还懂得如何

573
00:27:20.090 --> 00:27:22.750
bluff and to maintain emotional control.
虚张声势并保持情绪控制。

574
00:27:23.170 --> 00:27:23.930
Wow.
哇。

575
00:27:24.210 --> 00:27:24.910
Wowzers.
哇塞。

576
00:27:25.290 --> 00:27:25.970
It's a lot.
要求真多。

577
00:27:25.970 --> 00:27:27.270
It's a lot, lady.
要求真多啊，女士。

578
00:27:27.750 --> 00:27:29.470
You and Kevin can do all that.
你和凯文能做到这一切。

579
00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:32.490
I don't know if I can do all that all the time.
我不知道我是不是随时都能做到这一切。

580
00:27:32.830 --> 00:27:36.630
Well, I think this definitely supports, as Kevin, a secret genius.
嗯，我觉得这绝对证明了凯文是个深藏不露的天才。

581
00:27:36.930 --> 00:27:38.750
He's a lot smarter than he lets on.
他比表面上看起来聪明得多。

582
00:27:38.870 --> 00:27:41.190
And there are several more moments that show this.
还有好几个瞬间也能说明这一点。

583
00:27:42.450 --> 00:27:47.590
In Season 3, in the episode The Convict, we learn that Martin was in prison for insider
在第三季的《罪犯》那一集里，我们得知马丁因为内幕

584
00:27:47.590 --> 00:27:48.030
trading.
交易而坐牢。

585
00:27:48.030 --> 00:27:50.490
Kevin has a talking head where he says,
凯文有一段单人访谈，他说，

586
00:27:50.690 --> 00:27:56.630
I had Martin explain to me three times what he got arrested for because it sounds an awful
我让马丁给我解释了三遍他因为什么被捕，因为这听起来

587
00:27:56.630 --> 00:27:58.710
lot like what I do here every day.
跟我在这里每天做的事太像了。

588
00:27:59.970 --> 00:28:01.230
What's Kevin been doing?
凯文一直在干什么？

589
00:28:01.390 --> 00:28:04.070
Has he been trading Dunder Mifflin stock or other securities?
难道他一直在买卖邓德米夫林的股票或其他证券吗？

590
00:28:04.490 --> 00:28:05.870
I mean, what's happening?
我是说，这到底是怎么回事？

591
00:28:06.250 --> 00:28:07.570
Maybe some embezzlement?
也许有什么贪污行为？

592
00:28:07.870 --> 00:28:08.350
Maybe.
也许吧。

593
00:28:08.950 --> 00:28:15.330
Then there's the time Kevin outplays Andy and Daryl in the game Dallas in the garage sale episode.
还有一次是在车库出售那一集里，凯文在“达拉斯”纸牌游戏中击败了安迪和达里尔。

594
00:28:15.330 --> 00:28:16.050
Oh, yeah.
哦，对。

595
00:28:16.290 --> 00:28:16.590
Yeah.
没错。

596
00:28:17.070 --> 00:28:18.070
Let's hear that.
我们来听听那个。

597
00:28:18.690 --> 00:28:20.370
Well, there's no instructions.
嗯，这里没有说明书。

598
00:28:21.170 --> 00:28:21.810
It's Dallas.
这是达拉斯。

599
00:28:22.130 --> 00:28:22.590
The game.
这款游戏。

600
00:28:22.710 --> 00:28:23.270
We can figure it out.
我们能弄明白的。

601
00:28:23.430 --> 00:28:23.810
Yeah.
好啊。

602
00:28:24.130 --> 00:28:25.470
I'm pretty good at board games.
我很擅长玩棋盘游戏。

603
00:28:26.670 --> 00:28:28.710
Well, shall we make it a little interesting?
那我们来玩点刺激的怎么样？

604
00:28:29.070 --> 00:28:29.370
Sure.
行啊。

605
00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:33.240
Hmm.
嗯。

606
00:28:33.700 --> 00:28:36.860
I'm going to play me a little old blackmail card.
我要打出一张老牌的勒索牌。

607
00:28:37.180 --> 00:28:37.560
Nice.
不错。

608
00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:43.440
And call a proxy meeting and take control of Ewing Oil once and for all.
然后召集代理人会议，一劳永逸地夺取尤因石油公司的控制权。

609
00:28:43.440 --> 00:28:44.840
No, you can't do that.
不，你不能这么做。

610
00:28:45.200 --> 00:28:50.280
You can't play a blackmail card and call a proxy meeting in the same turn.
你不能在同一个回合里既打勒索牌又召集代理人会议。

611
00:28:50.760 --> 00:28:51.600
Why not?
凭什么？

612
00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:57.480
Seems to me we're just making up rules because somebody forgot to staple the rule book to
我觉得我们只是在临时瞎编规则，因为某人忘了把规则书像个正常人一样钉在

613
00:28:57.480 --> 00:29:00.120
the inside of the game like a normal human being.
游戏盒盖里面。

614
00:29:00.280 --> 00:29:03.900
And I'm going to play this here share the wealth card.
然后我要打出这张“均贫富”牌。

615
00:29:04.180 --> 00:29:04.380
Ooh.
哦。

616
00:29:04.660 --> 00:29:08.720
Which entitles me to half of both of you all's money.
这张牌让我有权平分你们俩一半的钱。

617
00:29:08.860 --> 00:29:10.560
So if you don't mind.
所以不介意的话。

618
00:29:10.760 --> 00:29:12.900
No, this card is from the wrong game.
不对，这张牌不是这个游戏的。

619
00:29:13.440 --> 00:29:14.960
This is from the game of life.
这是《人生》游戏里的。

620
00:29:15.380 --> 00:29:16.420
It was in the box.
它就在盒子里。

621
00:29:16.540 --> 00:29:17.440
Well played.
干得漂亮。

622
00:29:17.780 --> 00:29:17.920
Thank you.
谢谢。

623
00:29:17.920 --> 00:29:21.720
We must honorably adhere to the rules that we are making up on the spot.
我们必须郑重遵守我们当场瞎编的规则。

624
00:29:21.940 --> 00:29:23.340
No, but that's not fair.
不对，这太不公平了。

625
00:29:23.700 --> 00:29:24.800
Well, that's Dallas.
这就是达拉斯。

626
00:29:26.940 --> 00:29:27.820
Dallas, indeed.
确实是达拉斯。

627
00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:32.760
Okay, guys, it's my turn.
好了，伙计们，轮到我了。

628
00:29:33.060 --> 00:29:33.840
You can't go.
你不能走。

629
00:29:33.960 --> 00:29:34.180
You're dead.
你已经死了。

630
00:29:34.280 --> 00:29:35.220
